{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 440,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We follow one rule on Space Time: It’s never aliens. But every rule has an exception and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7100,
    "dDurationMs": 6519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this rule is no exception because: It’s\nnever aliens, until it is. So is it aliens yet?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19266,
    "dDurationMs": 5384,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "45 years ago, a pair of small Earth spacecraft inserted themselves into orbit around Mars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24650,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each dropped landing craft, which descended to opposite sides of the planet. This was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29260,
    "dDurationMs": 4819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Viking mission - the first successful\nMartian landing by the US. The landers performed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34079,
    "dDurationMs": 5241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiple experiments to investigate the Martian soil for signs of living microbes. In one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39320,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of these, samples were injected with a nutrient solution laced with radioactive carbon. The idea"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45210,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this labeled release experiment was that\nliving microbes would metabolize the nutrients"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50140,
    "dDurationMs": 2149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and emit radioactive gases."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52289,
    "dDurationMs": 6091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stunningly, both landers reported positive\nresults - 4000km apart from each other. As"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58380,
    "dDurationMs": 6710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a control, identical soil samples were first\nheated to 160 Celsius, in which case no response"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65090,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was observed - as you might expect if the\nmicrobes had been fried It seemed like a slam"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70290,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dunk detection of life on Mars. And yet no\nother Mars mission has managed to detect clear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76150,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "biosignatures, and today, the scientific consensus is that the labeled release experiment did in fact "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81730,
    "dDurationMs": 2350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not detect life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It seems that every few years for at least\nthe last half century we get another tantalizing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89760,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hint of the existence of alien life. But they\nall seem to fizzle out - at least, we never"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95020,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get the grand announcement that THIS particular evidence of aliens turns out to be aliens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101380,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what happened to all of this evidence.\nAre any of them still credible? And what's it going"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107110,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to take for scientists to decide that any\ngiven line of evidence adds up to proof. Or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111720,
    "dDurationMs": 3510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are scientists just too skeptical for that\nto even happen?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115230,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start - or rather continue with our\nquest to find evidence of primitive life in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119170,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our solar system. The Viking mission’s labeled release experiment sure looked like it had"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124120,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detected life. Except for one major difference. After the initial detection of these supposed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129550,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "microbe metabolites, additional shots of nutrients were injected. Now when this is done with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135310,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth samples, new bursts of gas are always observed as the microbes wake up and start"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141230,
    "dDurationMs": 2530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "feeding. But not so in the Martian samples."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143760,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Subsequent injections of nutrients yielded\nno response. This came to be interpreted as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148530,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evidence that the initial reaction was NOT\nbiological, but rather purely chemical."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154390,
    "dDurationMs": 5929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And NASA’s 2006 Phoenix lander found a possible culprit - perchlorate in the Martian soil."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160319,
    "dDurationMs": 5561,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This toxic chemical, or its byproduct hypochlorite, would be expected to break apart the organic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165880,
    "dDurationMs": 6079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compounds in the nutrients, producing gases that mimicked metabolites of biological activity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171959,
    "dDurationMs": 7191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And while microbes should multiply after feeding, a simple chemical like this would be depleted,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179150,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explaining the lack of response after the\nfirst nutrient injection. Hypochlorite should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183410,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also be destroyed by high temperatures, explaining the non-detection in the control sample."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188000,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s look as some of the other hints in our solar system. Still on Martian"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192300,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life, what ever happened to that supposed\nfossil microbe found in that Martian meteorite?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197420,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1996, scientists discovered elongated mineral structures that looked for all the world like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203240,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fossilized bacteria - but embedded deep in\na Martian meteorite that had been found in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208130,
    "dDurationMs": 6230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Antarctica. The scientists also found magnetite - a common byproducts of microbial metabolism"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214360,
    "dDurationMs": 6629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which at the time had no known abiotic source. This meteorite was special in other ways too - it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220989,
    "dDurationMs": 5481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had formed over 4 billion years ago, back\nwhen Mars had liquid water - in fact its geology"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226470,
    "dDurationMs": 6500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "required the presence of liquid water. This was a compelling enough hint at life that Bill Clinton made"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232970,
    "dDurationMs": 1670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a special TV announcement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234640,
    "dDurationMs": 6630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why don’t we count this as the discovery of life on Mars? Over the past couple of decades,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241270,
    "dDurationMs": 4859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "researchers have shown how these same structures - the “fossils” and the magnetite can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246129,
    "dDurationMs": 5581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be produced by the sort of chemical reactions you might have expected to have happened in this meteorite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251710,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s also difficult to rule out contamination\nby Earth microbes - after all, the thing had"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256530,
    "dDurationMs": 5049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been buried in Antarctica for millions of\nyears before geologists cut it open. No one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261579,
    "dDurationMs": 5781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has ever demonstrated convincingly that these are NOT Martian fossils - but they don’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267360,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to. When we’re talking about the discovery of alien life, the burden of proof rests very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273330,
    "dDurationMs": 6850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heavily on the shoulders of the discoverer.\nIt has to be implausible that the result could be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280180,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anything but life, and that’s just not the case with the would-be Martian fossils."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285840,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is the same reason we’re forced to reject - or at least be very cautious about - all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290600,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prior claims of evidence of alien life. For\nexample, the occasional spikes in methane"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297020,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detected by the Curiosity Rover on Mars could be microbe farts, but could also be any one of a number"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302530,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of geochemical processes. The red patterning on the surface of Jupiter’s moon Europa"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307660,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could be slime colonies, but could also be\nrust."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311000,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This brings us to Venus. In September 2020 Astronomers reported the signature of phosphine"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316940,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the upper atmosphere of Venus from radio data taken with ALMA, the Atacama Large Millimeter/submillimeter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322410,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Array. Phosphine is a common byproduct of biological activity on Earth, but it was difficult"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327810,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to come up with a non-living source of so much of it on Venus. This may have generated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332960,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most excitement since the Martian meteorite thing. Now, we went into the details soon after"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337940,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the announcement, so today I just want to\nanswer the question: what happened to this result?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344040,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, as you can imagine - for such a huge claim it sparked very careful scrutiny and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350460,
    "dDurationMs": 6170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "analysis. A few independent teams found issues with the original data processing, and their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356630,
    "dDurationMs": 7300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new analysis found little to no phosphine on Venus. The original team also re-did their analysis"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363930,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and now claim a seven-fold lower abundance of phosphine than in the original report - placing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369550,
    "dDurationMs": 3310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it at the level of a “tentative” detection."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372860,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the phosphine hasn’t quite gone away,\nbut even if it is actually present, it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377780,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now much easier to explain with non-biological processes. Once again - the burden of proof"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383310,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is on the new claim, and the evidence just\ndoesn’t cut it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387330,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a relevant quote from Sherlock Holmes on this point. \"When you have eliminated the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392790,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impossible, whatever remains, however improbable, must be the truth.\" There’s a certain Harvard"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399670,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Astrophysicist who’s fond of saying this in reference to his many claims that some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403770,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new astrophysical phenomenon might be aliens. And the statement is logically correct - improbable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409930,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "does beat impossible in terms of likelihood.\nBut it’s exactly this illusion of logic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416220,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that makes the Sherlock argument so misleading, and by far the most common cause of false"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422370,
    "dDurationMs": 7310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "declarations that it must be aliens cause it couldn't be anything else. The universe is weirder that we can ever imagine, and it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429680,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arrogant to assume you’ve thought of every possible non-alien phenomenon that could lead"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434190,
    "dDurationMs": 1910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to a particular observation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436100,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A great example of this is ‘Oumuamua - that weird object that flew through our solar system"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441030,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a few years back. ‘Oumuamua appeared as\na very faint, pulsing, reddish spot that slung"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446400,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around inner solar system just fast enough\nthat it most likely fell from interstellar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451250,
    "dDurationMs": 7070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space. Its pulsing was interpreted as being\ndue to an elongated object tumbling. Besides"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458320,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its interstellar origin, the weirdest thing\nabout it was the it appeared to accelerate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462530,
    "dDurationMs": 7449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on its way out of the solar system, \nin exactly the same way that comets do due to outgassing - ices vaporizing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469979,
    "dDurationMs": 5021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the Sun’s radiation acting like jets.\nIn the case of comets, this also results in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475000,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "classic comet tails, but Oumuamua had no tail. There was a claim by a,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480650,
    "dDurationMs": 5909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Harvard astrophysicist that the most likely explanation for Oumuamua is that it’s an alien artifact"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486559,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- in particular a light sail - probably a\nbroken one. Now, we covered all of this before"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490889,
    "dDurationMs": 6750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- both the discovery of Oumuamua and a clarification that there are huge problems with the alien hypothesis. But why"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497639,
    "dDurationMs": 6121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not aliens if there’s no accepted natural\nexplanation? Well the answer is simple: because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503760,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are more things in heaven and earth than are dreamt of in your philosophy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508020,
    "dDurationMs": 6500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And, lo and behold, since the publication\nof our video a far more reasonable explanation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514520,
    "dDurationMs": 5149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has been proposed. I’m actually going to\ndirect you to Anton Petrov’s excellent channel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519669,
    "dDurationMs": 5671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a really clear, in-depth analysis - but\nin short, Oumuamua is very well explained"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525340,
    "dDurationMs": 6490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a shard of an extra-solar ice world similar to Pluto, ejected into interstellar space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531830,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by an impact. Pluto also has a red tinge due to radiation processing its surface methane"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537970,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into hydrocarbons. This also explains the\nacceleration - it would be due to outgassing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543140,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the nitrogen-ice, abundant on such worlds. That would still act as a jet, but should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549340,
    "dDurationMs": 6601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be invisible. Now this may well not be the correct or entire explanation, but it does"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555941,
    "dDurationMs": 5879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make it very clear that there are perfectly\nplausible natural explanations for 'Oumuamua."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561820,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tabby’s Star is another good example of\nthis. Perhaps you recall - the crazily dimming"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565980,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star discovered by Tabitha Boyajian. Some\ndeclared that it’s best explained by an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571760,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alien megastructure eclipsing the star’s\nlight. That claim was on the strength that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576550,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no known natural phenomenon easily explained the observations. Since then, new observations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582300,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have shown that the obscuring stuff filters\nthe star’s light i n a way consistent with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587420,
    "dDurationMs": 6359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clouds of particulates - space dust, rather\nthan an opaque object. So we’re probably"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593779,
    "dDurationMs": 4831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looking at a tidally disrupted exoplanet or\nan evaporating exomoon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598610,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so all of this is a bit of a bummer if\nyou really want it to be aliens. Let’s get to "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603580,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some hints that don’t yet have satisfactory natural explanations. Many of you have heard"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610300,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the WOW signal. This was a weird spike\nof radio emission detected by the Big Ear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615370,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radio telescope at Ohio State University.\nThere was one idea that a pair of comets were"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621050,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the radio beam at the time, but the Ohio\nState astronomers insist that those comets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625390,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were not in the beam at all. No other straightforward, generally accepted explanation has emerged."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631380,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nor has such a signal ever been seen again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635570,
    "dDurationMs": 7060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or has it? On April 29th, 2019 the Parkes\nRadio Telescope in Australia was studying the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642630,
    "dDurationMs": 6370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flare activity of Proxima Centauri - our nearest stellar neighbor. A sharp spike of radio emission"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649000,
    "dDurationMs": 8540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appeared at 982.002 MHz and then drifted upwards in frequency over a 3 hour observation. This"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657540,
    "dDurationMs": 6890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weird signal was only spotted over a year\nlater when a student working with the Breakthrough"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664430,
    "dDurationMs": 5849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Listen project was re-analyzing the data.\nThere are lots of non-alien sources of radio"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670279,
    "dDurationMs": 5181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frequency blips - mostly human-made noise. But Breakthrough Listen has detailed protocols"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675460,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for sifting out these false signals. According to the program, this is one of a few candidates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681350,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that so far eludes natural explanation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684270,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This one is especially exciting because Proxima Cen was also recently shown to have a planetary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690420,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system, including a nearly Earth-mass planet it its habitable zone, where liquid water"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696120,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is possible. Now the Breakthrough Listen program hasn’t published its findings so we can’t say much - we know"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702630,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the increase in frequency is consistent with the Doppler shift of something accelerating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706640,
    "dDurationMs": 6590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "towards us. An invading fleet of Proximans? More plausibly from the motion of a planet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713230,
    "dDurationMs": 6750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around the star. Now there are very good reasons to think that this signal isn't aliens. For example,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719980,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Proxima is a dangerously active red dwarf\n- it’s unlikely that any planetary atmosphere"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724870,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could survive ts outbursts. It’s also so\nclose! If there’s a technological civilization"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731310,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on Proxima, that would suggest that there\nare many billions of them through the Milky"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735940,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Way, and many of them millions of years ahead of us. Which means we should have seen ample"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740880,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evidence by now. More likely this signal has a human source that somehow sneaked through the Breakthrough"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747420,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Listen protocols, or is a natural source\nthat we don’t know about yet. Or it could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752290,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be aliens - but no one’s claiming aliens\njust yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755670,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fact is, no matter now long we study the universe, it will always come up with natural"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760810,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "phenomena that we’ve never seen before.\nSo does this mean we can never conclude the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765200,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "existence of aliens based on any observation? Should we not even bother looking? Not at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770450,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all. I personally think that SETI programs should be robustly funded, and that we should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775240,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actively pursue the “It’s Aliens!” Hypotheses when warranted. The question is - when do"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781130,
    "dDurationMs": 4269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you run to the press shouting that it’s\naliens? Because you know the press is going"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785399,
    "dDurationMs": 4771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to go crazy over that - which is probably\nwhy you ran to the press in the first place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790170,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps the more useful question for most\nof us is - When do we believe some new hint"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795810,
    "dDurationMs": 7540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or claim of the detection of alien life? That’s easier to answer - optimistic skepticism is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803350,
    "dDurationMs": 7190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the way to go. Believe me, if it is aliens\nthe evidence will mount. Most scientists do want"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810540,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it to be aliens more than anyone, and will\nvigorously jump on board when it really does"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815810,
    "dDurationMs": 7010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "become more likely aliens than not-aliens.\nCurrently it’s not aliens. However planned missions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822820,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to Mars, Europa, Venus, and even the alpha-centuri system may finally prove that we’re not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828710,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the only living denizens in all of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832490,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As always, our deepest thanks to all of our\nPatreon supporters. Yes the money is really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837500,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "useful - this stuff costs a bit to make - but\nthe other thing that's incredibly helpful"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841690,
    "dDurationMs": 4829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is to feel your incredible support and enthusiasm. It's amazing to know there are so many people"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846519,
    "dDurationMs": 5481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who share our love for this stuff. Today's\nspecial Patreon shoutout goes to Leo Koguan,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852000,
    "dDurationMs": 5639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who's supporting us at the Big Bang level.\nLeo, as your special Big Bang Patreon reward"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857639,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we're going to reveal to you the true secrets of all those alien communications that astronomers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863079,
    "dDurationMs": 6211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have been hiding from the public for years.\nWe'll then introduce some nice Alpha Centaurians."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869290,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After that we have to neuralize you so you\nwon't remember the details. But deep down"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873530,
    "dDurationMs": 1999,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you'll know. You'll always know."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875529,
    "dDurationMs": 6191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re covering comments from the last two episodes: our update on warp drive theory,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881720,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the one about whether dark matter can be explained by enormous numbers of black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885590,
    "dDurationMs": 2689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes - tldw - it probably can’t."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888279,
    "dDurationMs": 6041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Aksel Randlepp asks whether understanding quantum gravity might be helpful for developing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894320,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "warp drives. Good insight. All this warp drive stuff is pure general relativity, and we know"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901480,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for sure that GR is not our final theory of\nnature because it doesn’t perfectly gel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905950,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with quantum mechanics. So maybe a theory of quantum gravity will reveal that warp drives"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911730,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are really possible, but the opposite is also\ntrue. Stephen hawking’s chronology protection"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917450,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conjecture states that the universe will always find a way to make time travel impossible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921140,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- and it’s been speculated that quantum\ngravity effects will come into play for things"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924820,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like warp drives, whose superluminal motion must allow time travel. A simple example is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930460,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the extreme curvature in the warp bubble walls should produce something like Hawking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935100,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiation, which may obliterate anything trying to ride in a warp bubble."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939520,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hall 9000 and RH2racing worry that warp travel is a non-starter unless there’s a way to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944990,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protect the ship from impacts because hitting even a tiny particle at those speeds would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949170,
    "dDurationMs": 4859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be devestating. So people have actually looked into this. It turns out that if a warp bubble"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954029,
    "dDurationMs": 4711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "runs into an object, that object will become\nfrozen at the leading edge of the bubble in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958740,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a similar way to how things appear to freeze at the event horizon of a black hole. Nothing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963820,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually passes into the bubble, so the ship\nis safe. When the ship reaches its destination"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968980,
    "dDurationMs": 5169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the warp bubble dissipates, everything\ncollected en-route would be released in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974149,
    "dDurationMs": 6081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forward direction. This would have so much energy on release that even just the space dust from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980230,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a short interstellar trip would become a beam of energy powerful enough to destroy a planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985810,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, yeah, don’t pull up right in front of\nyour destination, that’s rude; just park"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990570,
    "dDurationMs": 1459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around the side."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992029,
    "dDurationMs": 4961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nick Williams asks what the difference between super small black holes or naked singularities"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996990,
    "dDurationMs": 8529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and WIMPs be. Normally when people talk about wimps they’re referring to some undiscovered"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005519,
    "dDurationMs": 5291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum particle, but actually a micro black hole would have all the properties to qualify"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010810,
    "dDurationMs": 6950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it as a WIMP - namely wearing interacting,\nmassive, and particle-like. Fun fact: Einstein,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017760,
    "dDurationMs": 6919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "along with Nathan Rosen, tried to explain\nparticles as gravitational singularities - wormholes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024679,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between adjacent dimensions threaded by electromagnetic fields. Turns out particles aren’t that,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030939,
    "dDurationMs": 3921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it’s how the wormhole concept was invented in the first place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1034860,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Philippe Chaps correctly points out that there are scientists who have pushed the black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1039090,
    "dDurationMs": 6109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter hypothesis for years, so it hasn’t been overlooked as I implied at the beginning"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1045199,
    "dDurationMs": 5041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the episode. That’s fair - what I meant\nto say is that the hypothesis is not talked"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1050240,
    "dDurationMs": 4159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about much in the public sphere, hence the\nvideo."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1054399,
    "dDurationMs": 4951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Marc Abelha drops some wisdom from the opposite side of science - molecular biology. Which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1059350,
    "dDurationMs": 7319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that the answer often lies in plurality.\nDark matter might not be a single type of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066669,
    "dDurationMs": 5172,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing, but rather many different types of\nthing- like a dark standard model. Marc, your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071841,
    "dDurationMs": 4788,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "idea is so good that some very smart people already thought of it. For example, certain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1076629,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "types of supersymmetric counterparts to the standard model particles might serve as dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081149,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1082149,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Speaking of thinking along the same line as geniuses, many of you commented that you thought"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1086769,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the idea that dark matter is black holes\nyears ago. That’s impressive - you’re"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1091879,
    "dDurationMs": 4611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the company of some very smart people in that you’ve all been wrong about this for years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1096490,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark matter ain’t black holes. That may\nhave sounded unnecessarily snarky - and it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102570,
    "dDurationMs": 3609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wasn’t meant to be - it takes excellent\nintuition to come up with hypotheses that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106179,
    "dDurationMs": 6781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are even plausible. Actually being right is\na whole new level. Who knows, one day."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1112960,
    "dDurationMs": 6130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Keagan Wheeler-McCann offers a very nice addendum to Occam’s Razor: The simplest answer isn't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1119090,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "always the truth. But it doesn't, hurt to check. I like this a lot. Looking for dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124630,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter in, say, string theory, before you\ncheck whether it’s black holes is like looking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1129220,
    "dDurationMs": 5139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for your keys in the freezer before checking your pockets. And, actually, if dark matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1134359,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is enormous numbers of Planck-mass black holes, perhaps you could actually find your keys"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1139179,
    "dDurationMs": 5492,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AND dark matter if you checked your pockets. More on that soon."
    } ]
  } ]
}
